⚓ Pacific War 1: Admiral’s Tactical Manual
Winning in Pacific War 1 requires more than just firing salvos. You must master the "Information War," manage your fleet's positioning, and understand the mathematical risks of every engagement.

🏗️ 1. Fleet Composition & Roles
Each ship in your task force has a specific "job description." Misusing a unit is the fastest way to lose the war.

The Destroyer (The Greyhound)
Primary Role: Reconnaissance and Torpedo Denial.

Strategy: Use your 3-tile movement to "peek" around islands. Your goal is to trigger the Sonar Ping to turn enemy ? ghosts into active X targets for your Battleship.

Survival Tip: Never end a turn within 5 tiles of an enemy Battleship. Use your speed to move in, spot, and retreat behind an island.

The Battleship (The Sledgehammer)
Primary Role: Long-range Elimination.

Strategy: You are the only ship that can reliably hit targets from across the map. Position yourself in open water where you have clear lines of sight.

The Math: Because accuracy is P(h)=1−(d/R), staying at the edge of your 10-tile range gives you low hit chances. Move to mid-range (5-6 tiles) to maximize your Citadel Hit potential.

The Submarine (The Assassin)
Primary Role: Area Denial.

Strategy: Fire torpedoes into "choke points"—the narrow gaps between islands. Even if you don't hit, the AI will often move to avoid the torpedo path, forcing them out of their Repair Cycle.

🌪️ 2. Mastering the Information War
In this game, Knowledge = Range. ### Managing Radar Ghosts

Tracking Trajectories: When an enemy blip turns from X to x to +, look at the vector. If an enemy moved North for two turns, they are likely still moving North. Fire a "blind shot" at the predicted coordinate.

The Typhoon Blindness: During a Typhoon, your radar is almost useless. This is the time to stop moving. Engage the Repair Action and wait for the weather to clear. Let the AI come to you; they will likely stumble into your close-range detection radius.

The "Scout & Strike" Combo
Launch Air Recon (Carrier) at a suspicious cluster of islands.

Once the enemy is Identified (revealing their HP), focus all fire on the ship with the most Ammo remaining.

Removing the enemy's "teeth" (Ammo) is often more important than removing their HP.

🗺️ 3. Archipelago Navigation
The islands [#] are your best friends and your worst enemies.

Island Masking: Always try to keep an island between your Carrier and the center of the map. Carriers are large targets with high HP but are vulnerable to Citadel Hits.

The Ambush: Position a Destroyer or Cruiser right on the edge of an island. On your turn, move out, fire, and if you have moves left (Cruisers/Destroyers only), move back behind the cover. The AI cannot fire through landmasses.

🔧 4. The Logistics of Repair
The 3-2-2 Repair Rule is a "tempo" mechanic.

Knowing When to Sit: If a ship falls below 50% HP, it is in the "Danger Zone" for a Citadel Hit (which deals 4x damage).

Bait and Switch: Use a healthy Battleship to draw fire (Suppression), then move it behind an island to begin the 3-turn repair clock. While it sits, move your other units to cover its "blind" sectors.

The Repair Cap: Remember you can only heal to 75%. Don't waste turns sitting still if you are already at the cap; get back into the fight!

